item.text:
{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "P1A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §9 requires evidence of reliability/precision, reinterpreted here as consistency of AI scores across repeated evaluations. One score provides insufficient evidence, not evidence of instability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "gives no execution history",
          "reason": "The material does not establish whether repeated evaluations occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "one headline score of 75%",
          "reason": "One score cannot demonstrate variation across identical evaluations."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P1B",
      "instrument_rationale": "Under EFPA §9, explicitly absent repeat evaluations leave repeatability unassessed. They do not establish unstable performance.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "no repeat evaluations were conducted",
          "reason": "The record explicitly rules out measuring changes across repeated evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "this was the only evaluation performed",
          "reason": "Without repeated evaluations, instability across evaluations is not demonstrated."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§9.3 and 9.7 support examining repeated-score consistency. The report's three-point rule is a local criterion, not an EFPA rating threshold.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly describes repeated evaluations under the same conditions."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "73, 74, 74, 75, 74 and 73 percent",
          "reason": "The range is two percentage points, within the stated maximum of three."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P2B",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §9 calls for judging precision using an appropriate method and intended use. These repeats permit comparison with the report's stated stability rule, without assigning an EFPA coefficient rating.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identical inputs, settings and resources on six occasions",
          "reason": "The same conditions were used across six evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "51, 66, 79, 55, 82 and 62 percent",
          "reason": "The range is 31 percentage points, exceeding the stated maximum of three."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA separates documentation (§6.2), reliability evidence (§9), and item-content quality (§§6.1 and 7). Describing topics and scoring does not establish repeatability studies or answer errors.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It gives no execution history",
          "reason": "No supplied evidence establishes repeated identical-condition evaluations."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no findings about mistakes in the provided answers",
          "reason": "The material supplies no findings demonstrating answer mistakes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P3B",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §6.3.8 addresses software instructions and support separately from reliability (§9) and item quality (§6.1). Installation information adds no evidence on either claim.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "It reports no further evaluation results",
          "reason": "The appendix does not fill the missing execution-history evidence."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "no further evaluation results or examination of answers",
          "reason": "Neither the original document nor the appendix demonstrates answer mistakes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §6.1 distinguishes content-review procedures from resulting item quality. Independent review supports the development-process claim; reconciliation without agreement counts does not establish inter-rater agreement or error-free answers.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "Both questions and supplied answers underwent development review."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "does not state whether mistakes were found or how many remained",
          "reason": "Review completion does not establish correctness of every released answer."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P4B",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§6.1 and 7 distinguish review procedures from content quality. The review occurred, but identified released-answer errors directly limit answer quality; agreement before reconciliation remains unknown.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Two editors independently examined every question and supplied answer before release.",
          "reason": "The passage establishes review of both questions and answers during development."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "twelve supplied answers as wrong in the released version",
          "reason": "Explicitly identified wrong answers contradict universal correctness."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §6.3.2 requires transparency and checks of automated scoring correctness. Deterministic scoring establishes repeatability for identical text, not correctness. Under §9.6, inter-rater reliability is inapplicable to the described nonjudgmental scoring rule.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No comparison of those marks with independently established correct marks is reported.",
          "reason": "An unreported comparison cannot be assumed either to have occurred or not occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "identical text always receives the same mark",
          "reason": "Consistent marks can still be incorrect; universal correctness is not demonstrated."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P5B",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §6.3.2 distinguishes automated consistency from verified scoring correctness. Independent comparisons provide correctness evidence, and unresolved discrepancies conflict with universal correctness.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "tested the software against independently established correct marks for 20 chosen responses",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly establishes the claimed comparison."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Two marks differed and the discrepancies remain unresolved.",
          "reason": "Disagreements with independently established correct marks contradict correctness for every response."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §10 evaluates evidence for the stated interpretation and use. These counts support the narrow observed comparison. Under §8.3.2.2, norms need not be required for a comparison limited to ranking observed raw scores.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "identifying which system solved more of these 48 grid problems in this evaluation",
          "reason": "The reported counts directly answer this limited question."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "A solved more of the evaluated problems under the reported conditions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P6B",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §10 requires validity evidence proportionate to the breadth of the intended use. Results on these grid problems support the observed comparison but do not establish general intelligence across subjects and settings.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "No results from other tasks or settings are supplied.",
          "reason": "The evidence does not justify the stated generalization across subjects and real-world settings."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "A solves 36 and B solves 24.",
          "reason": "The narrow observed advantage is established regardless of the broader intended use."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§6.1.3.1, 3.2 and 10 connect scoring design to measurement aims and intended interpretations. Complete-grid scoring matches this explicitly restricted requirement.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "count only completely correct output grids",
          "reason": "One point for a completely correct grid and zero otherwise implements the stated requirement."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "partial correctness is deliberately outside this comparison",
          "reason": "Zero credit for a partially correct grid follows the requirement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P7B",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §§6.1.3.1 and 10 require scoring to match the stated measurement aim. Complete-grid scoring conflicts with a requirement to credit each correctly completed cell.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "give a response credit for every correctly completed cell",
          "reason": "The all-or-nothing rule withholds required credit for correct cells in partially correct grids."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "including partially correct output grids",
          "reason": "Giving such grids zero conflicts with the explicit partial-credit requirement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8A",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §9 distinguishes consistency across replications from other sources of variation, while §10.3.1.6 addresses experimental evidence. Here instructions vary, so the study concerns instruction changes rather than identical-condition repeatability.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No evaluation repeats an identical instruction.",
          "reason": "Instructions are part of the evaluation conditions and differ on every evaluation."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "each evaluation uses a differently worded instruction",
          "reason": "The study reports score changes while changing instructions and holding the other stated factors fixed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "P8B",
      "instrument_rationale": "EFPA §9 applies to repeated-score consistency under fixed conditions. Instruction-change effects would require varying instructions; this passage explicitly excludes that design.",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "claim1",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "software, resources, problem order, settings and instruction are identical on every evaluation",
          "reason": "The study reports score changes across evaluations with all stated conditions identical."
        },
        {
          "id": "claim2",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "No different instruction or presentation is studied.",
          "reason": "The study explicitly excludes instruction changes."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No material task instruction was unclear. The complete original EFPA 2025 model supplies the framework; case explanations cite relevant provisions rather than reproduce full reviews, as permitted. Test taker is reinterpreted as the evaluated AI, and repeated testing as repeated AI evaluation. Human bodily capabilities, human clinical classifications, guardian informants and human test-taker debriefing are inapplicable here; technical interface requirements and stakeholder reporting remain potentially relevant. Unmentioned norms, adaptations, reports and other properties are unassessed, not presumed deficient or inapplicable. Missing evidence would receive EFPA 0 where a rating is applicable, rather than 1 for demonstrated inadequacy; the requested claim labels are separate from EFPA ratings. No aggregate quality score or reviewer identity is assigned. Source: EFPA, Model for the Review, Description and Evaluation of Psychological and Educational Tests, version 5.0 (2025), supplied extraction, https://www.efpa.eu/, CC BY-SA 4.0. No external material was consulted."
}
